  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This Zenodo-hosted dataset shows early but real uptake: 288 downloads and 297 views indicate active use, and the record already includes 3 documented downstream reuse instances. The citation record is still very limited, with 0 citations in OpenAlex, so the impact story is presently centered on access and reuse rather than scholarly citation.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The <em>Archetypometrics dataset</em> is a green open-access dataset deposited in Zenodo in 2025 by Peter Sheridan Dodds of the University of Vermont. The repository description shows a structured release designed for reuse across workflows, with both MATLAB and plain-text versions available and separate archive and current versions maintained in the record.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest evidence of impact comes from use and reuse signals. DataCite reports 288 downloads and 297 views, which suggests the dataset has been repeatedly accessed. In addition, the reuse graph records 3 downstream reuse instances, including two text-based reuses and one biomedical article mention, indicating that the dataset is already circulating beyond its original deposit context.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Citation-based impact remains sparse at this stage. OpenAlex reports 0 citations, and the citing landscape is therefore not yet informative for field-level citation standing. That does not diminish the visible access and reuse activity; it simply means the scholarly citation record is still early.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The stewardship signals are also noteworthy. Zenodo provides persistent DOI-based access and broad repository coverage across disciplinary domains, while the record\u2019s related-identifier structure shows the dataset is part of a versioned sequence. Taken together, the evidence points to a well-curated dataset with emerging reuse and straightforward access, even though formal citation uptake has not yet accumulated.</p>",
